title: The socket options never reach the socket - LocalAddr, LocalPort and Reuse
  are dropped
updated: 2026-10-25T05:20:06Z
---

The connect helper creates a bare socket object, calls the socket method on it and connects itself. Every option of IO::Socket::IP is therefore dropped: LocalAddr, LocalPort, ReuseAddr, Type and Proto never arrive at the socket, and the failure is silent - the caller gets a connected socket and no indication that its options were ignored.

Verified: connecting with a fixed LocalPort leaves that port free afterwards, so the option was not applied. Only Blocking survives, and only because the module applies it afterwards by hand.

Work:
- pass the socket options through to the socket creation, so that LocalAddr, LocalPort, ReuseAddr, Type and Proto reach the kernel
- the happy eyeballs path may open several sockets for one connection - every attempt needs the same options
- the options that only make sense for the parent class, such as Listen, must not be silently accepted on a connection attempt
- add a test: connect to a local listener with a fixed LocalPort and assert that the port is taken afterwards; assert that the socket has the requested type
- the POD of the new method lists the accepted arguments - extend it accordingly

Commit 75feddc on offen-2610. t/12-socket-options.t red on old lib (LocalPort/ReuseAddr dropped, Listen accepted), green now; suite 76 tests. Decisions: forwarded options = LocalHost/LocalAddr/LocalPort/LocalService/ReuseAddr/ReusePort/V6Only; Type must be SOCK_STREAM (croak otherwise, a raced datagram makes no sense); Proto name/number feeds the getaddrinfo hints; Listen croaks in new(), and the -override leaves Listen calls to the original new. A fixed LocalPort can be bound by one attempt at a time (documented in POD).
